 ## Status
 
+**Mesh -> 3D-Gaussian-splat resampling.** The one item the phase-3 import work
+explicitly deferred ("not fit from a mesh — the design doc explicitly deferred
+mesh->3DGS fitting as its own research-grade problem"). `src/convert/
+mesh_to_gsplat.{h,cpp}` fits splats to a loaded `MeshAsset`'s surface and
+produces the same already-activated `GsplatAsset` `loadGsplatPly()` produces,
+so it reaches the existing `MATERIAL_GSPLAT` render path with no renderer
+changes at all — new `Representation::Splats` UI radio (with splat-count/size/
+opacity sliders), `--splats=N` CLI flag, `--splat-export=<path>` plus an
+`Export splats .ply` button, and `src/io/gsplat_ply_writer.{h,cpp}` for the
+export.
+
+Analytic surface placement, not an optimisation-based fit: splats are laid
+down area-uniformly over the triangles (stratified over an area CDF, Halton
+points within each triangle — no PRNG, so the same mesh and settings always
+give byte-identical output) as flat disks lying in the surface, oriented by
+the interpolated shading normal, coloured from the same linear base colour the
+mesh path shades with. A real gradient-descent fit against rendered views
+costs minutes to hours per asset, which fails doctrine (2) outright, and at any
+reasonable budget the analytic placement converges to the soft continuous
+surface such a fit would find anyway.
+
+The one number that had to be right is per-splat size, and it is derived
+rather than tuned: a splat contributes a 2D Gaussian of peak alpha `o` and
+std-dev `s` to the surface, integrating to `o*2*pi*s^2`, so at mean spacing `d`
+the accumulated alpha per unit area is `o*2*pi*(s/d)^2` and full coverage is
+`s/d = 1/sqrt(2*pi*o)`. That derivation is what makes the budget slider work
+across its whole range instead of at the one count a hand-tuned constant would
+have been tuned at — verified by sweeping 20k/200k/500k at fixed settings and
+confirming the surface stays continuous (no pinholes, no chalky fog) at all
+three while detail scales.
+
+**Fitting the mesh surfaced two pre-existing bugs in the phase-3 import path**,
+both invisible to that phase's own verification and both fixed here:
+
+- `__anyhit__gsplat` evaluated the Gaussian density *at the candidate hit
+  point*, which `__intersection__gsplat` reports on the 3-sigma shell by
+  construction — and that shell is exactly the level set where the density is
+  constant. So every splat's alpha was pinned at `exp(-4.5)` ~= 1%, regardless
+  of its opacity or where the ray actually passed, and a splat scene rendered
+  as a faint ghost you could see the background through (~90 overlapping splats
+  needed for one opaque pixel). Now evaluated at the ray's *maximum* response,
+  i.e. its closest approach to the splat centre — the standard ray-traced 3DGS
+  formulation (Moenne-Loccoz et al. 2024). This is the same failure mode as the
+  AgX `pow(2.2)` below: phase 3 checked "does it look like a soft alpha-blended
+  cloud?" against a scene that was *supposed* to be a soft cloud, and a
+  uniformly-1%-alpha bug looks exactly like that too.
+- The loader read `rot_0..3` as `(x,y,z,w)`, but the 3DGS convention is real
+  part first (`gaussian_model.py` initialises the identity as `rots[:, 0] = 1`
+  and `build_rotation()` reads `r = q[:, 0]`). Every imported splat therefore
+  had an extra 180-degree turn about X — invisible on the near-isotropic
+  synthetic sphere phase 3 was verified against, wrong for the strongly
+  anisotropic splats a real capture, or this fit, produces.
+
